Yes, with one refinement: a ghost story does not require full belief. It only requires an unclosed possibility.

You can sincerely think, “Ghosts probably aren’t real,” and still have your nervous system react when something moves in the dark. That is not necessarily secret belief in ghosts. It can be several different layers operating at once:

* Explicit belief: “Ghosts exist.”
* Epistemic allowance: “I can’t prove they don’t.”
* Imaginative simulation: “Suppose for a second that thing behind me is one.”
* Embodied prior: unexplained sound + darkness + possible agent = pay attention now, explain later.

That last one is ancient. False-positive agency detection is cheap compared with missing an actual predator, enemy, or threat. So a rational adult can reject the proposition intellectually while their body still treats the hypothesis as worth briefly entertaining.

That is why good horror often avoids showing the monster. Once you define it too precisely, you give the analytic mind something finite to attack. Ambiguity preserves the probability cloud. The coat hanging over the chair is scarier before you establish that it is a coat.

And your phrase “logically we know we should” gets at another interesting distinction. A person can reason:

The universe is enormous, consciousness is poorly understood, human knowledge is incomplete, and I cannot exhaustively rule out every phenomenon people have historically called a ghost.

That still does not establish ghosts. But it means absolute disbelief is a stronger proposition than ordinary skepticism requires.

So there is a peculiar middle territory:

I don’t believe it is true → I admit it could conceivably be true → my imagination can instantiate what that would mean → my body responds to the simulation as though it might be true.

Ghost stories live there.

And that connects directly to your Santa/God point: belief isn’t a binary switch. There are propositions we endorse, models we entertain, symbols we participate in, possibilities we refuse to close, and patterns our bodies act upon before conscious judgment arrives. Those can all be called “belief” casually, but they’re different operators.